---
title: Sensors
order: 2
description: Temperature, fan, and voltage monitoring
tags: [hardware, sensors, lm_sensors, hwmon, temperature, fans]
---

lm_sensors provides userspace tools for reading hardware monitoring
chips (temperature, voltage, fan speed) via the hwmon kernel subsystem.

@code bash
# Install lm_sensors
sudo pacman -S lm_sensors

# Detect sensor chips (interactive, run once after install)
sudo sensors-detect
# Answering YES loads the needed kernel modules
# Adds modules to /etc/modules-load.d/ for persistence

# Read all sensor values
sensors
# Output example:
# coretemp-isa-0000
#   Core 0:  +45.0 C  (high = +80.0 C, crit = +100.0 C)
#   Core 1:  +43.0 C  (high = +80.0 C, crit = +100.0 C)

# Specific chip
sensors coretemp-isa-0000
sensors -j                       # JSON output
sensors -u                       # raw values (for scripting)
sensors -A                       # no adapter type header

@text
Continuous monitoring and threshold configuration.

@code bash
# Watch sensors in real time
watch -n 1 sensors               # update every second

# GPU temperature (NVIDIA)
nvidia-smi --query-gpu=temperature.gpu --format=csv,noheader

# GPU temperature (AMD/Intel via hwmon)
cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*/temp1_input   # millidegrees Celsius

@text
The hwmon sysfs interface at /sys/class/hwmon/ exposes raw sensor data.
Each hwmon device has numbered attributes.

@code bash
# Browse hwmon devices
ls /sys/class/hwmon/
cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/name          # chip name

# Temperature (values in millidegrees Celsius)
cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/temp1_input   # current temp
cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/temp1_max     # max threshold
cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/temp1_crit    # critical threshold

# Fan speed (RPM)
cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/fan1_input

# Voltage (millivolts)
cat /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon0/in0_input

# List all hwmon readings in one shot
for f in /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*/name; do
  echo "=== $(cat "$f") ==="
  dir=$(dirname "$f")
  for t in "$dir"/temp*_input; do
    [ -f "$t" ] && echo "  $(basename "$t"): $(cat "$t")"
  done
done

@text
Sensor configuration lives in /etc/sensors3.conf and /etc/sensors.d/.
You can set labels, adjust offsets, and define virtual sensors.

@code bash
# Override labels and compute values in /etc/sensors.d/custom.conf
# chip "coretemp-isa-0000"
#   label temp2 "Core 0"
#   set temp2_max 85
#   set temp2_crit 95

# Reload configuration
sudo sensors -s
